            <ArticleTitle>Integrating intervention targets offered by homeostatic theory.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>2055102916634361</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1177/2055102916634361</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Marks presents &quot;homeostatic theory&quot; which proposes that weight gain is fostered by a &quot;Circle of Discontent&quot; consisting of body dissatisfaction, negative affect, and overconsumption. This innovative framework offers potential intervention approaches, including victim-blaming, stigma, and discrimination, as well as devalorizing the thin-ideal. Our article discusses possible ways that clinical health psychologists based in university settings may be uniquely positioned to consider and implement large-scale programs that have shown great promise for addressing these core issues.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
